.TH BLINKY 1 "2026-09-18" "blinky 1.7.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
blinky \- download and diagnose a SiPix StyleCam Blink II camera
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B blinky
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.I COMMAND
[\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B blinky
talks directly to a SiPix StyleCam Blink II (USB ID 0c77:1011) over its vendor
protocol, reproducing what libgphoto2's
.I camlibs/sipix/blink2.c
does, with retries, raw-data preservation, PNG output and a built-in
troubleshooter.
.PP
Stills are saved twice: \fIimageNNNN.raw\fR holds the exact byte stream from the
camera, written and flushed to disk before anything tries to interpret it, and
\fIimageNNNN.png\fR holds the decoded picture. Video clips need no decoding, so
\fIimageNNNN.avi\fR is itself the untouched raw data.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B doctor
Run five diagnostic checks in order, stopping at the first failure. Each failure
is explained in plain language with the likely fix. The checks are: the camera
is on the USB bus; the device opens without root; no other process holds it; the
handshake works; and the directory table is the expected size and internally
consistent.
.TP
.B list
Show the firmware ID, the number of photos, and the camera's directory table
with each entry's address range and size.
.TP
.B download
Download photos. Raw bytes reach the disk before any decoding is attempted.
.TP
.B update
Ask GitHub whether a newer release exists, and fetch it. The download is
checked against the SHA256SUMS published with the release and refused if it
does not match. With \fB\-\-check\fR it only reports; with
\fB\-\-install\fR it runs
.B sudo apt install
on the downloaded package.
.TP
.B delete
Erase photos from the camera. The protocol offers no per\-image delete: request
.B 0x12
erases everything and
.B 0x11
erases only the newest image, so those are the only two things this can do.
Always confirms before erasing unless \fB\-\-yes\fR is given, and refuses
outright when there is no terminal to confirm at.
.TP
.B decode " " \fIRAW\fR...
Decode saved .raw files to PNG. Needs no camera.
.TP
.B convert " " [\fIPATH\fR...]
Batch-convert .pnm files to PNG, verifying each result is pixel-identical to its
source. Never overwrites an existing file. Needs no camera.
.SH OPTIONS
These are accepted either before or after the command.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log every transfer.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress progress output. Results and errors still print.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " MS"
USB transfer timeout in milliseconds. Default 5000.
.TP
.BI \-\-retries " N"
Retries per image after the first attempt. Default 3.
.TP
.BI \-\-chunk " BYTES"
Bulk read chunk size. Default 4096.
.TP
.BI \-\-report " [FILE]"
Write the check results, the relevant kernel log lines,
.B lsusb \-v \-d 0c77:1011
and the tool's own log into one shareable text file. Auto-named if FILE is
omitted. If the kernel log cannot be read, blinky says so and carries on.
.SH "DOWNLOAD OPTIONS"
.TP
.BI \-\-out " DIR"
Output directory. Defaults to blink\-pics inside your Pictures folder, as XDG
reports it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-raw\-subfolder
Keep the .raw files beside the pictures. By default they go into a
.I raw/
subfolder, so the main folder holds only things you would want to look at.
Video clips stay with the pictures either way, the saved file being both the
raw data and the thing you play.
.TP
.BI \-\-images " SPEC"
Which photos, for example \fB0,2-4\fR. Default: all.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Re-download and overwrite existing files.
.TP
.BI \-\-formats " LIST"
Comma-separated pictures to keep for stills, from png, jpeg and bmp; default
\fBpng\fR, and at least one is required. png and bmp are lossless from the
decoded pixels, jpeg re-encodes data that is already JPEG.
.TP
.BI \-\-video\-formats " LIST"
Comma-separated videos to keep for clips, from avi and gif; default
\fBavi,gif\fR, and at least one is required.
.TP
.BI \-\-fps " N"
Frame rate recorded in a clip's AVI, default 5. The camera stores no timing
with the frames, but it records them at a known rate: SiPix's manual states
"approximately 3-4 frames per second" and the Blink II specification lists 5
fps for stored clips, so 5 plays a clip at roughly the speed it happened.
Raising it gives smoother but faster-than-real playback.
.TP
.BI \-\-jpeg\-quality " N"
JPEG quality, 1 to 100. Default 92.
.TP
.BI \-\-naming " continue|camera"
How downloaded files are named, default \fBcontinue\fR. The camera restarts
its numbering at zero every time it is erased, so its index is not an identity:
photo 0 today is a different picture from photo 0 last week.
\fBcontinue\fR numbers new photos on from the highest already in the output
folder, so names stay unique across sessions and keep sorting in the order the
photos arrived. \fBcamera\fR uses the camera's own index and adds a numeric
suffix when that name is taken.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-skip\-duplicates
By default a photo already present in the output folder is recognised by its
contents and not transferred again, even if the file has been renamed. This
turns that off and falls back to matching filenames only.
.TP
.B \-\-delete\-after
Erase the camera once the download is done. Because the camera can only erase
everything, this is refused unless every photo on it is accounted for: nothing
failed, \fB\-\-images\fR did not select a subset, and every file written has
been re-read from disk and checked byte for byte.
.SH "UPDATE OPTIONS"
.TP
.B \-\-check
Only report whether a newer release exists.
.TP
.B \-\-install
Install the download. This runs \fBsudo apt install\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-out " DIR"
Where to put the download. Default: a temporary folder.
.SH "DELETE OPTIONS"
.TP
.B \-\-all
Erase every photo. This is the default.
.TP
.BI \-\-last " N"
Erase only the N newest photos, by repeating the delete\-last request.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.SH "CONVERT OPTIONS"
.TP
.BI \-\-out " DIR"
Write PNGs here instead of beside the sources.
.TP
.B \-\-delete\-originals
Delete each .pnm once its PNG has verified as pixel-identical.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A check failed, a transfer failed, or a file could not be converted.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted.
.SH "OUTPUT LAYOUT"
Pictures are written at the top of the output folder, the camera's own bytes
into
.IR raw/ ,
clips into
.I videos/
and their animated GIFs into
.IR videos/gifs/ .
.PP
The raw files are always kept and cannot be turned off. They are the only copy
of what the camera holds: every other file is derived from them, duplicate
detection fingerprints them, and \fB\-\-delete\-after\fR verifies against
them before erasing a camera that cannot erase selectively. A picture cannot
stand in, having been decoded, de-interleaved and re-encoded into bytes that
bear no relation to the camera's.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-blinky-sipix.rules
Grants your login session access to the camera. Installed by this package.
.TP
.I ~/Pictures/blink-pics
Default output directory.
.SH "VIDEO CLIPS"
A clip is stored as Motion JPEG: complete JFIF frames laid end to end, padded
to 8-byte boundaries. It is not an AVI, despite libgphoto2 naming such entries
".avi" without inspecting them. Frames are 320x120 and carry the same
two-field interleave as a still, so each de-interleaves to 320x240.
.PP
blinky splits the stream, de-interleaves every frame and writes a real MJPEG
AVI at 5 fps, the rate the camera records at: see
.BR \-\-fps .
Rebuilding the container re-encodes each frame, which is a second lossy pass,
so the camera's own bytes are kept in the raw folder and
.B blinky decode
can rebuild the video from them at any time.
.SH "THE CAMERA FORGETS"
Pictures live in 8 MB of SDRAM, not flash, kept alive by the batteries. They
survive being unplugged from USB, but a flat or removed battery erases
everything and nothing recovers it. Download before changing batteries or
storing the camera, and treat a misbehaving link as a countdown rather than
an inconvenience.
.SH NOTES
This camera's USB link can be fragile. Errors such as \fB-71\fR (EPROTO) or
\fBdisabled by hub (EMI?)\fR in the kernel log are link-layer faults: the cable,
port or hub dropped the connection, and no software change will fix them.
.B blinky doctor
surveys the machine for free ports and explains what it finds.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gphoto2 (1),
.BR lsusb (8),
.BR journalctl (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Protocol documented by Marcus Meissner in libgphoto2's sipix camera library.
